{"ts": 1789771286.9509509, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "It clears, but only by 0.10 \u2014 and I should be honest with myself about what that means for my OOS forecast rather than just predicting 1.124.\n\n**Reasoning about the shrinkage:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~60 specifications and am reporting the maximum. The max of many noisy estimates is biased upward. With SE \u2248 0.22 per estimate and ~60 correlated draws, the winner's curse inflation is on the order of 0.15\u20130.3.\n\n2. **The bar itself is the tell.** The search-adjusted bar of 1.023 is the environment's estimate of what the best spec would score *from search alone* under a null. Exceeding it by 0.10 \u2014 less than half a standard error \u2014 is weak evidence of genuine edge beyond what searching guarantees.\n\n3. **Countervailing evidence for genuine signal.** Features 13, 22, 31 were the top individual performers *before* any combinatorial search, and each is individually positive. Feature 13 at 0.819 alone is ~3.7 SE from zero. The gains came from diversification across pre-identified strong features, not from dredging up an odd interaction. This is the good case.\n\n4. **The flat top.** Many triples clustered at 1.00\u20131.12. A flat likelihood means my specific pick isn't special; I likely picked the top of a noise distribution over near-equivalent specs. The true value is probably nearer that cluster's center than its peak.\n\nSo I'll shrink substantially toward the honest core signal, anchoring near feature 13's standalone performance plus a real but discounted diversification benefit, and use a wide SD reflecting genuine parameter uncertainty and possible regime shift."}
