{"ts": 1789771344.11328, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** Long features **13, 22, 31** (equal weight)\n- In-sample Sharpe: **1.124** (bar: 1.023)\n- Predicted OOS Sharpe: **0.78 \u00b1 0.42**\n\n### Method\n\nI swept all 40 features individually, then built combinations from the strongest. Feature 13 (0.819) and 22 (0.668) were the standouts; 31 (0.474) and 38 (0.457) followed. The winning triple is simply the three best standalone features combined \u2014 the diversification gain across imperfectly correlated signals lifted it from 0.819 to 1.124.\n\nI deliberately kept the search narrow (~60 evaluations). Grinding through all ~80,000 possible signed triples would have produced a higher in-sample number, but almost entirely through winner's curse, and would have made any OOS prediction less trustworthy.\n\n### Why I predict 0.78, well below the in-sample 1.124\n\nI shrank the estimate by about 0.35 for three reasons:\n\n- **Winner's curse.** With n=5000 the SE on any single Sharpe estimate is ~0.22. Reporting the max of ~60 correlated estimates carries meaningful upward bias.\n- **The margin is thin.** Beating a search-adjusted bar of 1.023 by 0.10 is less than half a standard error \u2014 weak evidence of edge beyond what searching alone would produce.\n- **The top is flat.** Many triples landed in 1.00\u20131.12, far tighter than the noise. When candidates are statistically indistinguishable, the one that comes out on top is likely sitting on a favorable draw.\n\nTwo things kept me from shrinking all the way to the bar: the component features were identified as strong *before* combinatorial search, and each contributes positively on its own with coherent sign behavior (the exact antisymmetry check confirmed the signs are doing something real). This is diversification across pre-validated signals rather than a dredged-up interaction.\n\nThe SD of 0.42 is wide on purpose. It spans roughly 0 to 1.6, so I'd be unsurprised if this washes out entirely \u2014 the honest read is a probably-real but modest edge that I cannot pin down tightly.\n\n**One note:** on my first tool call the environment reported that a number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Gmail, Google Drive, Linear, etc.) require OAuth authorization and can't be authenticated in a non-interactive session. This didn't affect the task \u2014 I only needed the `garden` tools, which worked fine. If you want those connectors available in future sessions, they'd need authorizing via your claude.ai connector settings, or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
